Author: Karuna G. Reddy [aut, cre] (<https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9467-4668>),
    M. G. M. Khan [aut]
Maintainer: Karuna G. Reddy <karuna.reddy@auckland.ac.nz>
Description: Implements simultaneous multivariate optimum stratification and
    sample allocation for complex survey designs. The package determines strata
    boundaries and sample allocation jointly across multiple stratification
    variables and multiple study variables, minimising a weighted composite
    variance objective subject to sample-size, cost (with fixed overhead), and
    coefficient-of-variation constraints. The core algorithm is a multi-start
    alternating optimisation scheme with monotone descent and formal
    convergence properties: boundaries
    are updated by derivative-free
    COBYLA or, under the parametric mode, by gradient-based SLSQP using the
    closed-form stratum-weight gradient; allocations are updated by an exact
    generalised Neyman (water-filling) formula. Stratum weights may be
    estimated empirically or analytically from thirteen parametric marginal
    distribution families, extending the univariate stratifyR framework
    (Reddy and Khan, 2020, <doi:10.1111/anzs.12301>) to the multivariate case.
    An automatic mode (distributions = "auto") selects the best-fitting
    family per stratification variable by AIC, optionally delegating to the
    FitVerse distribution-fitting package when installed.
    Includes three example survey populations, a design benchmarking tool,
    and a scientific visualisation function plot_strata() producing boundary
    diagrams for 1-D through n-D stratification spaces, including a 3-D
    perspective plot for the bivariate case. Operational helpers support
    precision-driven ("inverse") design via find_n(), which returns the
    smallest sample size attaining coefficient-of-variation targets on all
    study variables; selection of the number of strata via choose_H(); and
    deployment through assign_strata(), which scores an arbitrary frame, and
    as_svydesign(), which exports a ready-to-analyse design object for the
    'survey' package and 'srvyr'. A data-free mode, stratify_multi_density(),
    builds the design from the marginal densities alone (stratum moments by
    quadrature) when the stratification variables are the study variables.
    An interactive 'shiny' application bundled with the package (launched with
    run_app()) exposes the full workflow without coding.
